he Orange County Plain  Dealer who is very supportive of  prohibition and it's basically accusing  the City Council at that time of kind of  dragging their feet and they're not  really supporting that etc and basically  forces the City Council into passing an  ordinance a year before prohibition goes  into effect Nash nationwide into  basically drying out Anaheim now it  seems to very quickly happen that  Anaheim becomes quickly known as the  stronghold for the Klan one of the ways  you can actually see that is this  wonderful panoramic view that we have in  our collection that was taken in  probably about at the high point of the  Klan activity here in Anaheim and Orange  County it's not taken in Anaheim it's  actually taken at Huntington Beach one  of the things that I noticed first was  the fact that they're not hiding their  faces they're all exposed you will see  that the front of the crowd are women  and that there are actually a number of  young children that are dressed in the  regalia as well it's a significant  number of people that you're seeing in  this this group so it does lend support  to the fact that it was a large group  probably not in the thousands as was  sometimes claimed by the Klan but  certainly more than a couple hundred  people I would think to counter the Klan  activity if you will in Anaheim Lotus  Loudoun who is the publisher of our  other daily paper the Anaheim Bulletin  is publishing obviously editorials  they're promoting their own candidates  for office they're trying to you know  again the other side of the coin we're  not we are following the letter of the  law we are supporting prohibition you  know for example here's one of their  newspapers and basically he's saying  their pastor expresses Klan smokescreen  dry issue to mislead voters so they're  basically using again the Anaheim  bulletin as their voice to counter what  the Klan is basically accusing the old  the old families of you know that  they're you know really not you 